ividends he's not even trying  he's gone straight for  hammering working people and  last week i was speaking to rosie she's  a care worker  and this double whammy is going to mean  hundreds of pounds more for her and tax  87 pounds a month she's going to lose on  universal credit she's asked for more  shifts she can't get them she's got  child care issues it's people like rosie  care workers retail workers hospitality  workers who are getting hammered by this  government and on universal credit would  you keep that uplift permanently is that  something you put in your next manifesto  we've been fighting the government on  this uplift month in months out it is  wrong to remove that thousand pounds  uplift it will impact millions of  families who will be poorer because of  it  in the long run we would reform  and change universal credit because it  doesn't work well  particularly when people start to want  to earn a little bit more money they get  trapped in the system so we reform it in  the long run but  absolutely keep this uplift at the  moment it's a lifeline for millions of  families and would you what about the  social care levy that the government is  introducing is that something you would  keep to fund social care long term we  think there's an alternative which is to  fund this  in a broader way where those with the  broadest shoulders pay their fair share  and to put all of the burden on working  people  is wrong it's unfair and the thing about  this tax is it's a tax rise it's unfair  because it hammers working people and  it's not going to fix the problem we  don't even know when this money is  actually going to be paid  on social care at all  and it only addresses one small part of  the problem which is whether people have  to sell their homes  many people with moderate homes will  still have to sell their homes  the much bigger questions of  providing care  for people in their own homes paying the  workforce properly left completely  unaddressed by th